          <p>
            The bigger price gap is between product styles, not between casein and whey. Isolates and certified powders cost more: ISO100 runs about 18 cents more per 25 g than ON whey, and Ascent's Informed Sport casein about 29 cents more than ON casein. Over a month of one scoop a day, that is roughly $5 to $9 extra, which is worth paying if you need the certification or the lower lactose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tub size moves the number too. Small 2 lb tubs nearly always cost more per gram than 4 or 5 lb ones, and prices swing week to week with sales and Subscribe and Save offers. For cheaper options across all protein types, see our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide, and our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> rankings for the strongest picks on each side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Should You Take Both, or Buy a Casein and Whey Blend?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You only need both if you will use each for its own job: whey after training or during the day, casein before bed. For most people one powder is enough, and whey is the more flexible single choice. Buying two tubs doubles the outlay and often means one sits half-used.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the effect of both in one tub, a blended protein mixes fast and slow proteins. Products in this style combine whey concentrate or isolate with micellar casein or milk protein, so part of the scoop absorbs quickly and part slowly. Our batch review of <a href="/syntha-6-protein-powder-review/">Syntha-6</a> looks at one well-known example. Blends make sense for people who want one powder they can take at any time, including before bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also make your own blend. Half a scoop of whey and half a scoop of casein in the same shake gives a thicker drink with both release speeds, and the mix bakes better than whey alone. Plain milk does a similar job: it is roughly 80% casein and 20% whey, which is why a glass of milk before bed is a cheap alternative to a casein shake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who should pick which</h3>

          <p>
            Lifters on a budget who want one tub should pick whey, ideally a big tub of ON Gold Standard. Lactose-sensitive buyers should pick a whey isolate. People who train in the evening, older adults trying to add protein, and anyone who struggles to eat enough during the day should add casein before bed. Tested athletes should choose a powder with a sport certification, such as Ascent's Informed Sport casein; our <a href="/ascent-protein-powder-review/">Ascent protein powder review</a> covers the full range. If you are also weighing up creatine, our <a href="/creatine-vs-protein-powder/">creatine vs protein powder</a> comparison explains why they do different jobs and stack well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand by brand, our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> and <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> cover the other products in each line, and the <a href="/compare/">product comparison tool</a> lets you put any two powders side by side on label figures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying calcium caseinate for slow release</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not all casein is slow. Micellar casein keeps its natural structure and clots in the stomach; calcium caseinate and hydrolyzed casein digest faster. If a powder just says casein, or lists caseinate first, it may not give the overnight release you are paying for. Check the ingredient list for micellar casein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking casein builds more muscle overnight on its own</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Snijders trial compared nightly protein with a no-protein placebo, so the protein group simply ate more protein. When daily protein is matched, timing studies find little difference. A bedtime casein shake helps if it raises your daily total; if you already eat plenty, it adds little.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking casein like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Casein in a shaker with a little cold water often turns thick and lumpy, which is behind many complaints in buyer reviews. Use more liquid, around 300 ml per scoop, a blender, or let it sit for a minute. Or lean into the texture and make a pudding with a few tablespoons of milk instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming casein is lactose-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lactose is milk sugar, and micellar casein still carries some. People with lactose intolerance sometimes switch from whey to casein expecting relief and get the same bloating. A whey isolate is usually the lowest-lactose dairy option. Anyone with a true milk protein allergy should avoid both and talk to a doctor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per tub instead of per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2 lb casein bag at $45 looks cheaper than a 5 lb whey tub at $106, but per 25 g of protein it costs more. Divide the price by servings times protein per serving, then multiply by 25. At the prices we saw, ON's whey and casein were within 2 cents of each other.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein or whey better for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey produces a larger short-term rise in muscle protein synthesis, thanks to faster absorption and more leucine, per Tang et al. 2009 and Pennings et al. 2011. Over months of training, total daily protein matters far more than type. Most people should use whey as their main powder and add casein only if a bedtime shake helps them reach their daily protein goal.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I take whey and casein together?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Mixing half a scoop of each gives a thicker shake with both fast and slow release, and many protein blends do exactly this. There is no harm in combining them, and no special benefit either beyond convenience and texture. Count both toward your daily protein. If you buy only one, whey is the more flexible choice for most routines.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does casein take to digest compared with whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey causes a sharp, short rise in blood amino acids, while casein produces a moderate plateau that lasted most of the seven-hour test period in Boirie et al. 1997. The exact time depends on dose and what else you eat. Casein is often described as releasing amino acids for several hours, which is why it is marketed for overnight use.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Casein can fit a weight-loss diet, but it is not more filling than whey in the best-known test. In Hall et al. 2003, people ate less at a buffet 90 minutes after whey than after casein. Casein's advantage is texture: a thick pudding can replace a dessert. For fat loss, the key is enough total protein while eating fewer calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I drink casein every night?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only if it helps you reach your daily protein target or suits your schedule. Research from Maastricht University shows 30 to 40 g of casein before sleep is digested normally and supports overnight muscle building, but when daily protein is matched, bedtime timing shows little extra benefit. If you already eat enough protein, a nightly shake is optional rather than essential.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does casein have more calories than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No, they are about the same. Optimum Nutrition's casein and whey both give 24 g of protein in roughly 120 to 130 calories per scoop, per the labels at the time of writing. Isolates such as Dymatize ISO100 run slightly lower, around 110 to 120 calories. Flavor changes the figures a little, so check the label of the exact tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein safe for everyone?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Casein is a normal milk protein and is safe for most healthy adults. People with a milk protein allergy should avoid it, because casein is a common allergen. Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, or who takes medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding protein powder. Our guide to whether protein powder is safe covers testing and contaminants.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

